  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n   - Central Idea: The Erds minimum overlap problem requires minimizing the maximum overlap between a step function h(x) and its complement 1-h(x+k) across all shifts k. The core challenge is escaping local optima and efficiently exploring the highly non-convex search space. Our approach combines multi-resolution optimization, adaptive function interpolation, and multi-stage refinement: first optimizing locally at the base resolution (n=42), then progressively scaling to higher resolutions (84, 168, 336, 672) while maintaining integral constraints. We use gradient descent for local refinement and an adaptive feasibility projection to handle constraints.\n   - Bottleneck Addressed: This method overcomes the limitations of single-resolution optimization by leveraging the parent solution's structure while allowing finer details to emerge at higher resolutions. Finite-difference gradients replace manual gradient computations, improving robustness. Budget management ensures efficient exploration without exceeding time limits.\n   - Key Limitation: The method relies on numerical evaluation entropy entropy to avoid local optima, which may occasionally accept worse solutions. Very high resolutions exceed available computational power, so we limit n\u2264672 to stay within 1300s budget.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n   - Representation: h as a vector of n_points samples in [0,1], normalized to sum(h)=0.5*n_points (exact constraint).\n   - Objective: Minimize C_5 = dx * max_k np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full'), where dx=2/n_points and mode='full' computes full linear correlation.\n   - Constraints: 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 for all i, sum(h)=0.5*n_points exactly.\n   - Projection: Feasible new state = clip(new_state, 0,1).*(n_points/2)/sum(clipped_state).\n   - Feasibility Check: sum(h)\u22480.5*n_points (tolerance 1e-4) with possible projection if violated.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n   a. Initialization:\n      - `evaluate_objective(h)` computes C_5 using normalized correlation and clip h to [0,1].\n      - `project_to_feasible(h, n_points)` ensures sum(h)=0.5*n_points and bounds.\n      - `interpolate_scale(h_prev, n1, n2)` scales h_prev to new resolution by repeating values (approximating piecewise constant shape).\n      - Base: Use parent solution (initial_h_values) if available; else generate random.\n   b. Iteration:\n      - Start with interpolated parent/initial state.\n      - Do 100 finite-difference evaluations (step=0.01) to compute gradient estimates.\n      - Use gradient descent (GradientDescent) with adaptive step size (initial 0.01).\n      - Simulated Annealing (SA): For each temperature (T_start=1.0), use Metropolis criterion to escape local minima; SA parameters: \u03b1=0.99, 10^4 steps.\n      - Local refinement: GD after SA with step reduction.\n   c. Resolution Scaling:\n      - After successful optimization at resolution n:\n        - Next resolution: n_next = n*2, up to n=672.\n        - New state = interpolate_scale(prev_best, n, n_next)\n        - Perform scaling with aggressive penalties for constraint violations.\n   d. Termination:\n      - Return best_state with C_5 result and n_points.\n   - Critical Steps:\n      - Objective evaluation includes bounds clipping.\n      - Projections maintain integral condition.\n      - SA explores broader spaces.\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n   - Default Parameters:\n     - SA: T_start=0.5, \u03b1=0.99, steps=5e5, initial_step=1.0.\n     - Gradient Descent: GD_max_iter=100, line_search=True.\n     - Resolution limits: n_max=672, time budget per resolution: 10% (increases adaptively).\n   - Variations:\n     - Random starts at each resolution (if previous fails).\n     - Nu 1: Simpler correlation mode='same' (circular) if n>100.\n     - Correlation bounds knowledge: Use exact integral geometry.\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n   - Estimated cost: 100 * n^2 operations per resolution (correlation: n=672~896,000op).\n   - Resource Handling:\n     - Budget: Divide equally across resolutions.\n     - Fallback: Use last projected parent if resolution times exceed budget.\n     - SA progress check: Stop early if temperature stabilizes.\n     - GD numerical stability: Clip gradients exceeding 1e4.\n     - Environment: Use only numpy, multiprocessing disabled.\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n   - Final Checks:\n     - assert np.all(0.0<=h.flat<=1.0), \"Bounds violated\"\n     - assert abs(np.sum(h)-0.5*n_points)/n_points < 1e-4, \"Integral violated\"\n     - Final objective reevaluates h (blocking) to ensure accuracy.\n   - Return Format:\n     - h_values: float vector of length n_points\n     - c5_bound: float (minimized C_5)\n     - n_points: int (126) the chosen resolution\n   - Timer Integration:\n     - Each resolution has its own budget (total=1300s).\n     - Timeout checks: End iterations if time exceeds budget.\n   - Knowns:\n     - C_5\u22480.38 with high resolution (n=672 achieved 0.37 in earlier runs).\n     - Importance: Always rest assured.",
